\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The Green's function $G_{\gamma }(0,y)=y^2 g_{\gamma }(0,y)$ for three different values of $\gamma $: $4+\gamma /H=5^2$ (black), $4+\gamma /H=7^2$ (dashed) and $4+\gamma /H=10^2$ (dotted), the first peak of $G_{\gamma }$ is around $y=5,\,7,$ and $10$, respectively, supporting the fact that the location of the peak is at $\sqrt  {4+\gamma /H}$.}}{59}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{GreGamma}{{6}{59}{The Green's function $G_{\gamma }(0,y)=y^2 g_{\gamma }(0,y)$ for three different values of $\gamma $: $4+\gamma /H=5^2$ (black), $4+\gamma /H=7^2$ (dashed) and $4+\gamma /H=10^2$ (dotted), the first peak of $G_{\gamma }$ is around $y=5,\,7,$ and $10$, respectively, supporting the fact that the location of the peak is at $\sqrt {4+\gamma /H}$}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The Green's function $G_{\gamma }(x,y)$ (on the top) and its temporal derivative, per Hubble time, $\partial _tG_{\gamma }(x,y)/H=-x\partial _xG_{\gamma }(x,y)$ (on the bottom); for $x^\star =\sqrt  {4+\gamma /H}$ as a function of $y>x^\star $ (on the left), and for $y^\star =\sqrt  {4+\gamma /H}$ as a function of $x<y^\star $ (on the right). All plots correspond to $x^\star =y^\star =10$.}}{59}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{GreGamma2}{{7}{59}{The Green's function $G_{\gamma }(x,y)$ (on the top) and its temporal derivative, per Hubble time, $\partial _tG_{\gamma }(x,y)/H=-x\partial _xG_{\gamma }(x,y)$ (on the bottom); for $x^\star =\sqrt {4+\gamma /H}$ as a function of $y>x^\star $ (on the left), and for $y^\star =\sqrt {4+\gamma /H}$ as a function of $x<y^\star $ (on the right). All plots correspond to $x^\star =y^\star =10$}{figure.7}{}}
